(1) The implementation of services for women and children victims of violence shall be carried out in an integrated manner by the PPT.

(2) The PPT as referred to in paragraph (1) constitutes an integrated part and is unified with the PPT that implements integrated services for witnesses and/or victims of human trafficking crimes.

(3) The implementation of services for women and children victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) includes: a. providing fast and free services to victims; b. implementing protection and fulfillment of victims' rights to health rehabilitation, social rehabilitation, repatriation, social reintegration and legal assistance;

c. cooperating with certain institutions in providing translators and assisting volunteers needed for victims;

d. networking with government or private hospitals for treatment and health recovery of victims and cooperating with witness and victim protection agencies, social protection homes or trauma centers owned by the government, community or other institutions for health recovery of victims;

e. providing convenience, comfort and safety for victims;

f. maintaining the confidentiality of victims; and

g. providing fulfillment of legal assistance for victims.

(4) Further provisions regarding procedures and mechanisms for implementing the PPT shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

CHAPTER V

RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE Article 45

Every woman and child victim of violence shall receive the following rights:

a. the right to complaint handling;
b. the right to have their dignity and worth as human beings respected;
c. the right to receive protection from family, community, regional government and/or other parties either temporarily or based on protection orders from the court;
d. the right to health and psychological recovery from suffering experienced by victims;
e. the right to determine their own decisions;
f. the right to special handling related to victim confidentiality;
g. the right to obtain information;
h. the right to confidentiality of their identity;
i. the right to social rehabilitation;
j. the right to assistance by social workers and legal aid at every level of examination process in accordance with laws and regulations; and
k. the right to spiritual guidance services.

Article 37

Forms of handling of women and children victims of violence include:

a. complaint, consultation, and counseling services;
b. assistance services;
c. medical rehabilitation services;
d. social rehabilitation services;
e. legal services; and
f. social reintegration services.

Article 46

Women and children victims of violence, in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in Article 45, shall also receive special rights, as follows:

a. the right to survival;
b. the right to grow and develop;
c. the right to receive equal protection; and d. the right to participate in community life.

Article 39

Assistance services as referred to in Article 37 letter b include:

a. accompanying victims during examination and health recovery processes;
b. accompanying victims during examination processes at the police, prosecutor's office and court;
c. monitoring the interests and rights of victims in examination processes at the police, prosecutor's office and court;
d. maintaining the privacy and confidentiality of victims from all unauthorized parties, including reporting by mass media; e. providing a sense of security to victims; and
f. providing continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage.

Article 24

(1) The implementation of Protection and empowerment of children in the field of social welfare as referred to in Article 13 letter d, the Regional Government is obliged to organize social welfare for:

a. children in conflict with the law;
b. child victims of violence who are exploited economically and sexually, neglected children, child victims of kidnapping and child victims of human trafficking;
c. child victims of abuse of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropics and other addictive substances (NAPZA);
d. children infected with HIV/AIDS;
e. children who do not have parents and are cared for by other parties or families who are unable;
f. street children;
g. child victims of natural disasters or social disasters;
h. children with disabilities;
i. children of migrant worker families;
j. children who live in or around prostitution locations; and k. child victims of other maltreatment.
(2) Social welfare as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of service provision: a health; b education; c legal assistance; d assistance; e social, mental and spiritual guidance; f social rehabilitation; g empowerment; h social assistance; and/or i reintegration.

